{:edb/db-before-t 1415 :edb/db-after-t 1416 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534728 50 #inst "2026-10-12T09:31:55.507Z" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073447 1071 "Which of the following ratios is equivalent to $12 : 9?$\n\nI. $2 : 3$\n\nII. $4 : 3$\n\nIII. $5 : 3$" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073447 1071 "Which of the following ratios is equivalent to $12: 9$?\n\n1. $2: 3$\n2. $4: 3$\n3. $5: 3$" 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186073447 1074 "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3$. So our equivalent ratio is of the form $0$\n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12: 9$, we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}}$, so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3$.\n\n$$\n12\\begin{vmatrix}: & 9 \\\\ ↓ \\div 3 & ↓ \\div 3 \\\\ [math]\\phantom{0}[/math] &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nSo to get $0$ from ${\\color{blue}{12}}$, we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186073447 1074 "All of the answer choices have the second number equal to $3.$ So our equivalent ratio is of the form ![](images/35/35577ae6ed7f13a59f140d75fbd066251ec539423a5de48daa1736e7bbec25f8.svg)  \n\nTo find a ratio that's equivalent to $12 : 9,$ we divide both numbers by the same value. In particular, we need to get $3$ from ${\\color{red}{9}},$ so we divide ${\\color{red}{9}}$ by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So to get ![](images/21/211a836d739a9a5eadbaa452ad307a4d01d32818d1bd2ccee48eec256f64a2f0.svg) from ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ we divide $12$ by $3$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}12} \\div 3 =4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12:9$ is equivalent to $4:3.$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073448 1074 "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $4$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $6$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to total shapes is $4:6$.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in the ratio by $2$:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 2 &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to the total shapes is $2:3$." 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186073448 1074 "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $4$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $6$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to total shapes is $4:6.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in the ratio by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to the total shapes is $2:3.$" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073449 1071 "![](images/8d/8d9f0a004ca9beb84b3a2a604d71dbf43ab70a67d899b167c5183ecc2ebe15f3.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\n> The ratio of squares to circles is $0$ because there is $1$ square for every $0$ circles." 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186073449 1071 "![](images/8d/8d9f0a004ca9beb84b3a2a604d71dbf43ab70a67d899b167c5183ecc2ebe15f3.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is ![](images/29/296da85a5c6d0f5cf14f8fe144b0cdf66e4082b425b886d98fcbe672a2e8a190.svg) because there is $1$ square for every ![](images/b1/b1fda7079b1445faec7a1a2bb2abcafd9147f8a28863a2618c66166cea102a14.svg) circle(s)." 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073449 1074 "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $2$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $2$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of circles to squares is $2:2$.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 2 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 1 &:\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:2$ is equivalent to $1:1$.\n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:\\bbox[3px,lightgray]{1}\\,$ because there is $1$ square for every $\\bbox[3px,lightgray]{1}\\,$circle.\"" 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186073449 1074 "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $2$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $2$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of circles to squares is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:2$ is equivalent to $1:1.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:\\bbox[3px,#d3d3d3]{1}\\,$ because there is $1$ square for every $\\bbox[3px,#d3d3d3]{1}\\,$circle.\"" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434315 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073738 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434316 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434313 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434317 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434314 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434318 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186073972 1050 420.0 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186075034 1064 "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5$, for example, we get the ratio $10:30$.\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\cdot 5 & ↓ \\cdot 5 \\\\ 10 &:\\end{vmatrix}30\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent.\n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2$, we get $1:3$.\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}: & 6 \\\\ ↓ \\div 2 & ↓ \\div 2 \\\\ 1 &:\\end{vmatrix}3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $1:3$ are also equivalent." 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186075034 1064 "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186075035 1064 "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186075035 1064 "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n$$\n0\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3: 5$, we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value.\n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}}$, so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4$.\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}: & 5 \\\\ ↓ \\cdot 4 & ↓ \\cdot 4 \\\\ 12 &:\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]\n$$\n\nSo, to get $0$ from ${\\color{red}{5}}$, we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20$." 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186075036 1064 "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio:\n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9$, so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n18\\begin{vmatrix}: & 9 \\\\ ↓ \\div 9 & ↓ \\div 9 \\\\ 2 &:\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1$.\n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." 13194139534728 17592186045417 false] [17592186075036 1064 "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434285 1080 "6" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434285 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434285 1083 #uuid "0462ac67-14b3-5481-a443-957e97903cf8" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434286 1080 "4" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434286 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434286 1083 #uuid "05bfa6a3-b567-5a5f-afbb-e5591b3e1ac6" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434287 1080 "2" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434287 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434287 1083 #uuid "189ca1fc-d9bf-54e1-943e-8d472270ae3f" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434288 1080 "1:2" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434288 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434288 1083 #uuid "25b4050d-2855-552a-a59b-c2b4ec9e988f" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434289 1080 "2:1" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434289 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434289 1083 #uuid "26f3ce87-0441-5aab-b938-80ad045e3822" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434290 1080 "5:8" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434290 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434290 1083 #uuid "2eb20220-add6-5735-a22a-3c908dc65462" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434291 1080 "2" 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [17592186434291 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534728 17592186045417 true] [175